m print a diagnostic  when handed a format that is not theirs.  pnmtosir/sirtopnm come back the right size with the channels rotated  over the first half of the pixels -- a solid red 4x1 returns as green,  green, red, red -- so for a round trip use another format.  The cause  is two bytes of header: pnmtosir writes 1536 shorts of header, three  aligned blocks making 3072 bytes, and sirtopnm skips 1535 of them, so  it starts on the pixels two bytes early and every plane it reads is  shifted by two.  That is the whole of it -- the reader's skip loop --  and it is as the 1991 original has it.ppmtopict/picttoppm round-trip exactly -- PICT joins the list above.picttoppm warns `can't load font directory' and converts anyway.  ppmtoyuvsplit/yuvsplittoppm round-trip the size but not the pixels,  which is the format itself: 4:2:0 chroma subsampling keeps one  luma byte per pixel and one chroma pair per 2x2 block, so a 32x16 image  gives .Y = 512 bytes and .U = .V = 128.  ppmtoyuvsplit takes a base name  and writes three files in the data directory, not to standard output.  sputoppm reads any bytes as an Atari Spectrum 512 picture and writes a  320x200 PPM -- the format is a fixed size with no magic number to check,  so unlike the nineteen converters that answer `not a <format> file' it  accepts anything.  imgtoppm writes nothing for a format that is not  its own.  pbmtobbnbg reads standard input and ignores a filename handed to it as  an argument, so `pbmtobbnbg b.pbm' waits on the terminal and calls  whatever comes next `bad magic number - not a pbm file'.  Redirect  instead -- `pbmtobbnbg < b.pbm > o.bg' -- and it writes the BitGraph  escape sequence: ESC P, the size, the packed rows, ESC \.  Into the PNM formats (49)  asciitopgm     reads a text picture -- ASCII art -- as a greymap, each                 character's darkness a grey level  atktopbm       reads an Andrew Toolkit raster as a PBM  bioradtopgm    Bio-Rad confocal microscope