Basic 1.10 for the 68020 -- the same demonstration as                 `ub68kdemo' and it runs the same way, announcing `OS9/68020                 Version' where the other says 68000.  sddemo         White's Speedisk 2.10 -- disk defragmenter.  Wants an                 80x24 screen; falls back to tty mode  ub68kdemo      UniBasic 1.10 -- a BASIC compiler, demo build.  Run it                 from a copy of DOC/unibasic, where its library and examples                 are; -c stops at the C it writes, and a full build needs                 your own OS-9's cc  Compiling with UniBasic, start to finish.  It works; here is what it  wants.  UniBasic translates your BASIC into C, then drives the C  compiler to build a module, so it needs your OS-9 C toolchain:    These four steps are in the OS-9 shell's own language -- `setenv'    and `chd' are its commands, not bash's.  In bash use `export    NAME=value', and note that bash's `cd' does not move the OS-9    data directory at all (DOC/README-RUNNING says why).    1. Set SHELL.  UniBasic runs its compile through $SHELL.  With SHELL       unset it looks for `/dd/bash' and stops with `Error Exit' and       error 216, so set it:       setenv SHELL /dd/CMDS/sh    2. Work where basic.h and basic.l are.  chd to a directory holding       both, or copy them beside your program.  DOC/unibasic has them.    3. Point at your C toolchain.  cc, c68, o68, r68, l68, cpp and merge       on the execution path; setenv CDEF to your headers and CLIB to       your libraries.  The link wants cstart.r, clibn.l and sys.l.    4. Give it room.  The preprocessor says `No more memory !!!' if it       is short; os9exec users want something like -M 128M -mm 8M.  A five-line program written from scratch, compiled by ub68kdemo, ran  and printed its output.  The compiled  bench1..bench11 programs ship beside their sources, so there is  something to run before you set any of this up.  bio, blackjack and wysetime are BASIC09 I-code, and want runb.------------------------------------------------------/dd/CMDS/MM1 -- device drivers for the MM/1, and a terminal (8)------------------------------------------------------  Drivers and descriptors for the MM/1, one of the machines OS-9/68000  ran on.  These are modules you load, not programs you run; start  one from a shell and it reports that it could not begin, as a driver  should.  Kept because an MM/1 is a real OS-9 machine and somebody still has  one.  On anything else they have no hardware to drive.  keydrv.mm1     the MM/1's keyboard driver  msdrv.901_340  the MM/1's mouse driver  msdrv_340.901.ms  the MM/1's mouse driver, a second build of the same edition  rb37c65        the MM/1's floppy-disk driver, for its 37C65 controller  scsi_mm1a      the low-level SCSI routines the hard disk driver links to  snddrv         the MM/1's sound driver  windio.52      the MM/1's windowing terminal driver  vt100          a VT-100/VT-52 terminal program for the MM/1 under                 K-Windows: vt100 /t2.  Alt-/ is its menu (hang up, shell,                 options); no file transfer of its own -- shell out to                 kermit.  Untested: there is no MM/1 here------------------------------------------------------/dd/CMDS/ADL -- the Adventure Definition Language (4)------------------------------------------------------  ADL is a language for writing text adventures -- Ross Cunniff and  Thomas Brengle's, 1986-87, from EFFO forum disk 3.  You write a world in  ADL, compile it, and the runtime plays it.  /dd/ADL holds what came with it: standard.adl, the definitions that make  a game behave the way "The ADL Player's Handbook" describes; AARD, a  full adventure with its source and a makefile; MPU, another; and four  small demos under DEMOS -- tiny, baby, actdemo and multi.      adlcomp <file>.adl -o <world> -i <dir>   compile a world      adlrun  <world>                          play it      adldebug <world>                         the same, with the debugger      adltouch <file> <number>                 re-date a saved game  The -i is not optional for anything that includes standard.adl,  which is most of it: the include names it without a path, so adlcomp  needs telling which directory to look in, and it takes absolute  paths throughout:      adlcomp /dd/ADL/DEMOS/tiny.adl -o /dd/tmp/tiny -i/dd/ADL      adlrun /dd/tmp/tiny  -- 0 errors, 2 warnings, a 10548-byte world, and it opens in a small  but comfortable room with two pillows in it.  AARD is already built  and needs none of that: `adlrun /dd/ADL/AARD/aard'.  adlcomp       compile an ADL world  adldebug       the ADL debugger: loads a compiled world with its symbol                 table and dumps its tables -- objects, nouns, verbs,                 routines, strings -- over a range of numbers. ? lists the                 commands, q leaves  adlrun         plays a compiled ADL world at a > prompt -- look, take,                 inventory, a direction; save and restore keep a game, quit                 leaves. AARD is ready to run in ADL/AARD: `adlrun aard'  adltouch       re-dates an ADL save file: `adltouch <save> <n>' writes n                 over its first four bytes, the stamp that ties a saved game                 to the world it was saved from------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------/dd/CMDS/NEWS -- C News and rn (12)------------------------------------------------------  C News, Geoff Collyer and Henry Spencer's, University of Toronto,  1985-1990, ported to OS-9 by blarson.  It is the transport and storage  half of Usenet; rn, below, is the newsreader that goes with it.  The archive is called GNU_ATP_1_40.lzh and is not GNU and not ATP; its  own README begins "This is C News, ported to os9/68k by blarson@usc.edu".  Another mislabelled name in that archive.  Terms are permissive: "Permission is granted to anyone to use this  software for any purpose on any computer system, and to alter it and  redistribute it freely", subject to four conditions about credit and not  misrepresenting it.  DOC/cnews/COPYRIGHT is the whole notice, with the  README, ROADMAP and 14 manual pages.  relaynews, C News's relay, files articles into SPOOL/CNEWS; its  configuration is USR/LIB/CNEWS.  rn, Larry Wall's newsreader, reads that  spool, and Pnews posts to it.  One file of each was changed for this  disk's directories (SRC/cnews/README.OSK, SRC/rn43/README.OSK).  newsrun feeds relaynews the batches waiting in SPOOL/CNEWS/in.coming.  It calls blarson's `trlf', which is in no archive; the trlf here was  written for this disk to do the two jobs newsrun gives it.  *bdecode       decodes a C News batch: it skips forward to the line `Decode                 the following with bdecode', decodes what follows and checks                 the CRC at the end. Given anything else it says `Missing                 header'. Source in SRC/cnews/input *byteflip       reorders the bytes of each word on standard input, so a dbz                 database moves between architectures: `byteflip 4 0 1 2 3 4                 3 2 1 0' reverses four-byte words. Give it the numbers: with                 none it reads zero bytes for ever  *c7decode      the inverse of C News's c7encode: it reads the seven-bit-safe                 form a news batch is put into to cross a link that eats the                 eighth bit, and writes the eight-bit original back. Source in                 SRC/cnews/input  *dbz           builds and maintains C News's history index -- the .dir and                 .pag pair beside the history file that lets the news system                 find an article by message-id without reading the whole of                 it. `dbz database [file]...'   newsetup      writes a .newsrc for rn from the groups in the active file,                 each marked unsubscribed until you choose.  rn runs it for a                 newcomer, through your own OS-9's `shell'; run directly it                 needs nothing  *newshist      looks message-ids up in the news history and reports what it                 finds, or that there is no entry for them: `newshist                 "<id@site>" ...'. -df names a history file other than the                 system's *newslock       the news system's lock: `newslock <tempname> <lockname>'                 links the temporary name to the lock name and returns 0;                 while the lock exists it fails, returning 1  newsrun        feeds the batches in SPOOL/CNEWS/in.coming to relaynews one                 by one, uncompressing and converting line endings, and                 removes each once filed; refused ones go to in.coming/bad.                 `-v' reports each. `load trlf' first; it uses your own                 OS-9's `shell'   Pnews         posts an article to C News: it asks for the distribution,                 newsgroup and subject, or with -h <file> -s sends a file that                 has its header.  The post waits in SPOOL/CNEWS/in.coming for                 relaynews   relaynews     C News's relay: files an article or a batch under its                 newsgroup, numbers it, updates the active file and the history,                 and refuses a Message-ID it has seen.  `relaynews -r -n <                 file'; -r sends its log to USR/LIB/CNEWS  rn             rn 4.3, the newsreader: `rn <group>'; y reads, space pages,                 q leaves. Needs a .newsrc (newsetup writes one) and a RAM                 disk at /r0 (`mount -r=256k /r0'), or it stops and leaves                 .newsrc renamed .oldnewsrc. Manual in DOC/rn   trlf          turns LF into CR, for newsrun: `trlf <file>' converts the file                 in place, `trlf -s' standard input to standard output.  A file                 already in OS-9's form is unchanged.  Written for this disk------------