e cio, which ships in /dd/CMDS.  All of them  want a serial port to dial out of, which is what they are here for.  The whole package is GPL v2; /dd/DOC/uucpbb/copying is the licence  as it included. *adduser        adds a user to the system, or removes one with -r *dotilde        the tilde-escape handler mailx and postnews call for each `~'                 line typed into a letter: `dotilde <command> <t2flag> <uid>                 <quotechar> <letter> <homedir> <message>' -- ~r appends a                 file, ~m the message replied to, ~h lists the rest *expire         delete news articles past their expiry date *fileserv       the UUCP mail file server: reads a message on standard input                 and obeys `reply <address>', `help', `get <file>', `dir' and                 `quit' in its body, mailing answers back through rmail.                 Serves SPOOL/files; logs to LOG/FileServ *fixtext        repair the line endings of a received text batch *mailx          reads and sends mail: `mailx' opens what is waiting (-r                 newest first), `mailx <address>' sends, -a names a file                 being replied to. It wants a mailbox directory of its own,                 not elm's mailbox file, and says so when there is none *postnews       post an article to a newsgroup: it asks for the header, reads                 the body, and hands the article to rnews, which files it in                 SPOOL/news. It takes the Distribution from                 SYS/UUCP/distributions *readnews       read Usenet news articles: it opens the reader and asks                 about each newsgroup in the active file not yet in                 .newsrc, then answers `**** End of newsgroups' when the                 news spool is empty *rmail          delivers incoming mail into a directory per user -- uuxqt                 runs it, not a person. MAIL names the directory that holds                 the users' mailbox directories, and each letter