es)', and all ten DVI drivers then read  it.  The .dvi and .log land in the DATA directory.  `dvips' wants a header file tex.pro -- point it at one, or use one of  the ten dvi* drivers; `dvialw' writes PostScript too.  Read /dd/DOC/tex/Installation.text and Usage.text first -- they are the  port's own, and they explain building a format and making fonts.  There  are worked documents in SYS/TEX/SAMPLES, and About_Latex.tex is a LaTeX  article describing this very system.  bibtex         BibTeX, the bibliography formatter. It prompts for the job                 name on standard input (`Please type input file name'). The                 .bst styles plain, unsrt, abbrv and alpha are in                 SYS/TEX/INPUTS  dvialw         turns a TeX .dvi file into PostScript for an Apple LaserWriter  dvidjp         turns a .dvi file into output for an HP DeskJet Plus  dvieps         turns a .dvi file into output for an Epson 9-pin dot-matrix printer  dviimp         turns a .dvi file into imPRESS for an Imagen laser printer  dvijep         turns a .dvi file into output for an HP LaserJet Plus  dvijet         turns a .dvi file into output for an HP 2686A LaserJet  dvilj2         turns a .dvi file into output for an HP LaserJet II  dvimac         turns a .dvi file into output for an Apple ImageWriter (144 dpi)  dvioki         turns a .dvi file into output for an Okidata 192 dot-matrix printer  dvitos         turns a .dvi file into output for a Toshiba P-1351 dot-matrix printer  dvitype        prints what is inside a .dvi file, as text  gftopk         MetaFont generic font to packed font  gftype         show what is inside a .gf file  inimf          MetaFont with no base preloaded  initex         TeX with no format preloaded, for building .fmt files  latex          LaTeX -- Lamport's document preparation system on top of TeX  pktogf         packed font back to generic font  pktype         show what is inside a .pk file  pltotf         property list to TeX font metric  slitex         SliTeX -- LaTeX for slides  tangle         WEB to Pascal -- Knuth's literate-programming tool.  Name a                 change file too, always: DOC/tex has none.ch, an empty one,                 beside sample.web -- `tangle sample none'  tex            TeX itself -- the typesetting program (a driver; virtex does the work)  texidx         sorts the \indexentry lines in a LaTeX .idx file into the                 index LaTeX reads back. This build stops with `virtual                 memory exhausted' on any input, and no source is here to                 rebuild it  tftopl         TeX font metric to property list (the readable form)  vftovp         virtual font to virtual property list  virmf          the real MetaFont engine -- generates fonts from .mf sources  virtex         the real TeX engine, loaded with a format  vptovf         virtual property list to virtual font  weave          WEB to TeX -- the other half of literate programming, and                 it needs a change file for the same reason `tangle' does:                 `weave sample none'  maketexpk     generate a .pk font at the size TeX asked for  dvips          DVI to PostScript, dvipsk 5.495b, the fuller of the two                 PostScript drivers (dvialw is the other): `dvips <file>.dvi                 -o <file>.ps'. A font size not yet built is left blank; `-M'                 stops it offering to make one. Source: SRC/dvips  afm2tfm       turns an Adobe .afm font metric file into the .tfm TeX reads------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------/dd/CMDS/DHRY -- the Dhrystone benchmark, twelve builds (12)------------------------------------------------------  Dhrystone 2.0, the classic integer benchmark, built twelve ways from one  source.  The point is the comparison: dhry is the Microware cc build, *dhryGcc and dhryGcc2 are GCC 1.x and 2.x, and the O2, in, mx and shamu  suffixes are optimisation and inlining variants.  Running several on the  same machine tells you what the compiler costs you.  On real hardware this measures your machine.  Run under an emulator it  measures the host instead, which is still worth having if you have a  figure from real hardware to set beside it.  Source is in SRC/dhry.  dhry           Dhrystone 2.0 built with Microware's cc. It reads the number                 of runs from standard input: `echo 500000 | dhry'. Twelve                 builds of the same source sit in CMDS/DHRY, so the compilers                 can be compared *dhryGcc        Dhrystone 2.0 built with GCC 1; see dhry *dhryGcc2       Dhrystone 2.0 built with GCC 2; see dhry *dhryGcc2in     Dhrystone 2.0 built with GCC 2 with inlining; see dhry *dhryGcc2mx     Dhrystone 2.0 built with GCC 2, mixed options; see dhry *dhryGcc2o2     Dhrystone 2.0 built with GCC 2 at -O2; see dhry *dhryGccin      Dhrystone 2.0 built with GCC 1 with inlining; see dhry *dhryGccmx      Dhrystone 2.0 built with GCC 1, mixed options; see dhry *dhryGcco2      Dhrystone 2.0 built with GCC 1 at -O2; see dhry  dhryO2         Dhrystone 2.0 built with Microware's cc at -O2; see dhry *dhryshamu      Dhrystone 2.0 built with GCC 1 at -O2 with inlining, strength reduction and no frame pointer; see dhry *dhryshamu2     Dhrystone 2.0, a second shamu build; see dhry------------------------------------------------------/dd/CMDS/GCC139 and /dd/CMDS/GCC2 -- the gcc toolchains (15)------------------------------------------------------Two generations of GNU C for OS-9/68k.  Microware's cc is the compiler foralmost everything on this disk; gcc is here because a few programs needANSI C, which Microware's K&R cc cannot parse.  ls is the one on this diskthat was built this way.Read DOC/README-GCC first.  gcc2 searches /dd/CMDS/gcc_<pass> and gppsearches /dd/CMDS/gpp_<pass>, both hardcoded rather than /dd/CMDS/GCC2, sothe five passes those two drivers look for are installed in /dd/CMDS underthe names they ask for:    `gcc_cccp2'  `gcc_cc2'  `gpp_cccp'  `gpp_cc1plus'  `gpp_collect'They are byte copies of cccp2, cc2, cccp2 again, cc1plus and collect from/dd/CMDS/GCC2 -- cccp2 twice on purpose, because gcc2 forks it as cccp2and gpp forks it as cccp.  The originals stay where they are.  GCC139 isnot installed this way; its gcc_cc1plus and GCC2's would want one name.The programs:  gcc           the GCC driver -- GCC139's and GCC2's share this name.                They are not the same version, and neither is 2.x:                GCC139/gcc answers `gcc version 1.39' and GCC2/gcc                answers `gcc version 1.42', read out of `gcc -v' *gcc2          the GCC 2.x driver, and the only one that is:                `gcc version 2.5.6'  gpp           the C++ driver -- and it is a GCC 1.x one.  GCC2/gpp                says `gpp version 1.40.3 (based on GCC 1.40)' and                GCC139/gpp says 1.37.1  gcc_cc1       GCC 1.39 C compiler pass  gcc_cc1plus   GCC139's C++ compiler pass: `GNU C++ version 1.37.1'  gcc_cccp      GCC 1.39 preprocessor  gcc_cccp2     GCC 2.x preprocessor, under the name the gcc2 driver forks it by  gcc_cc2       GCC 2.x compiler pass, under the name gcc2 forks  gcc_collect   GCC 1.39 collect2  gpp_cccp      GCC 2.x preprocessor, under the name gpp forks  gpp_cc1plus   GCC2's C++ pass, 1.40.3, under the name the gpp driver forks it by  gpp_collect   GCC 2.x collect2, under the name gpp forks  cc1plus        a GCC C++ compiler pass (1.40.3) in the GCC2 directory; the                 driver runs it  cc2            the GCC 2.x C compiler pass, in the GCC2 directory; the                 driver runs it, and -version reports it  cc2plus        a second GCC C++ compiler pass (2.5.8) in the GCC2 directory *cccp2          the GCC 2.x preprocessor, in the GCC2 directory beside its                 driver  collect        collect2: builds the table of global constructors and                 destructors a C++ program needs before linkinggcc compiles to assembly and then needs r68, l68, clibn.l and cstart.r,Microware's.  With your SDK, both compilers work -- C and C++, compiled,linked and run.Where a compile reaches, on a four-line hello.c:  cccp2 file.c file.i        preprocesses.  Works.  gcc2 -S -o file.a file.c   compiles to 68k assembly.  Works, and the                             assembly is Microware's syntax -- `psect',                             `link.w a5,#0', `bsr _stkcheck'.  gcc2 -o file file.c        stops: `Can't fork \'r68\': 000:221 module                             not found'.So everything up to the assembler is here and works; the assembler andlinker are Microware's.  GCC139/gcc forks a module named `os9exec' andfails earlier -- use gcc2.  GCC139 (6) gcc gcc_cc1 gcc_cc1plus gcc_cccp gcc_collect gpp             Use CMDS/makeinfo (GCC139/makeinfo was the same file).  GCC2   (8) cc1plus cc2 cc2plus cccp2 collect gcc gcc2 gpp------------------------------------------------------/dd/CMDS/GCC137 -- GNU C 1.37.1, the CERN distribution (3)------------------------------------------------------GNU C 1.37.1 for OS-9/68000 as CERN distributed it in February 1991, withits source in SRC/gcc-1.37.1-osk.  It compiles for the 68000 and for the68020 with or without a 68881, and hands its assembly to your own r68 andl68.  Its headers are in /dd/GCCDEFS and its gnulib.l in /dd/GCCLIB,where it looks for them.  -B names where the passes are:    gcc137 -B/dd/CMDS/GCC137/gcc137_ -I/h1/DEFS -L/dd/GCCLIB -L/h1/LIB -o hello hello.cIt asks for a resident `cc1' and `cccp' before it looks where -B points,and GCC139's passes go by those names and stay resident once run: afterusing GCC139, `unlink cc1 cccp' first.  DOC/gcc137 has the distribution'sREADME, the port's notes (Doc), the history and the manuals. *gcc137        the GCC 1.37.1 driver: `gcc version 1.37.1' *gcc137_cccp   GNU C 1.37.1's preprocessor *gcc137_cc1    its C compiler pass: `GNU C version 1.37.1 (OS-9/68000)'------------------------------------------------------/dd/CMDS/GCC272 -- GNU C 2.7.2 (5)------------------------------------------------------GNU C and C++ 2.7.2 for OS-9/68000, ported in 1995-97 -- the newest gcc onthis disk.  Its source, as far as the archive carries it, is inSRC/gccsrc272; SRC/gccsrc272/README.OSK says what is missing.  It compilesfor the 68000 through the 68060 and CPU32, and hands its assembly to yourown r68 and l68.  Its support library is GNULIB/libgcc000.l (020 and 300for the other CPUs); -uwlibs links it with your own clibn.l.The driver forks its passes by bare name, so each is pointed at its ownfile with the driver's -F option, and GCC2_OPTS holds default options:    export GCC2_OPTS="-Fcccp2x[/dd/CMDS/GCC272/gcc272_cccp2] -Fcc2x[/dd/CMDS/GCC272/gcc272_cc2] -Fcc2plusx[/dd/CMDS/GCC272/gcc272_cc2plus] -Fcollectx[/dd/CMDS/GCC272/gcc272_collect]"    gcc272 -uwlibs -L/h1/LIB -o hello hello.cC and C++ both compile, link and run; C++ programs link without a streamlibrary.  DOC/gcc272/readme.osk is the porter's ownguide to the options and search rules.  gcc272         the GNU C and C++ 2.7.2 driver: `gcc2 version 2.7.2'  gcc272_cccp2   the preprocessor, for C and C++  gcc272_cc2     the C compiler pass: `GNU C version 2.7.2'  gcc272_cc2plus  the C++ compiler pass: `GNU C++ version 2.7.2' *gcc272_collect  builds the table of global constructors and destructors                 a C++ program needs; gcc272 runs it for every link------------------------------------------------------/dd/CMDS/DRIVERS -- drivers and file managers for a real system (10)------------------------------------------------------  Modules for an OS-9/68000 kernel: file managers, drivers, device  descriptors, and a cache utility with its trap handler.  You load them,  or put them in a bootfile; they are not programs, and started from a  shell they say so.  WE CANNOT TEST ANY OF THESE -- the emulator this  collection is checked on loads no file managers or drivers -- so what  is written here comes from their own documents and code.  Each has its  source in SRC.  ptyman         PtyMan 1.3, a pseudo-terminal file manager: one program                 opens /pty/<name>, another /tty/<name> and gets an echoing,                 line-editing terminal joined to it; any number of pairs.                 osknet's telnet server uses it. DOC/ptyman/ptyman.doc  ptydrv         PtyMan's placeholder driver; its descriptors name it  pty            the descriptor for PtyMan's server side, /pty  lfcrman        a file manager that shows another device with its line                 endings translated between LF and CR, for sharing text                 with a Unix machine over NFS.  From OS-9 International                 2/93  h0t            an example lfcrman descriptor: /h0t is /h0, translated  thlpct         an example lfcrman descriptor: /thlpct is /thlpc, translated  disp           an SCF driver written in C, the example from OS-9                 International 1/93, "Writing a device driver in C": it                 puts the low four bits of each character on a display                 port.  An example to adapt  di             the descriptor for the disp example driver, port $FEC30000 *cpucache       shows and switches the 68020/68030/68040 on-chip caches.                 Its driver is not here, so of what it does only `cpucache                 -q on|off' works without one of your own  cctrap         the trap handler cpucache calls to read and switch the caches------------------------------------------------------/dd/CMDS/X68K -- programs for the Sharp X68000's window system (2)------------------------------------------------------  OS-9/X68000 ran on Sharp's X68000 with a windowing system of its own,  the personal window, reached through /win.  WE CANNOT TEST THESE:  there is no X68000 here, and each stops at the window it cannot open.  DOC/README-X68K has what their Japanese documents say; the archives  themselves ship whole in CMDS/archives, as their terms require. *x9eyes         xeyes for the personal window, resizable *setbgptn       sets the window system's background pattern from a file------------------------------------------------------/dd/CMDS/SYSADMIN -- logins, messages, modems, cron and an error log (15)------------------------------------------------------  System programs from TOP, The OS-9 Project's second release (Munich,  1989-90), for an OS-9 machine that several people log into, some of  them over modems.  WE CANNOT TEST WHAT THEY ARE FOR: that needs serial  lines, users and a password file, and users logged in at once, whom  logon records in SYS/utmp.  Most answer -? with their usage, which is  where most of their documentation lives; DOC/SYSADMIN has manuals for  logon, mmon, watch, mesg, mmenu, msg, newgrp, speak and uid in proff  format, utmp.prf on the SYS/utmp routines, and a sample entrytypes and  mmon.config. *logon          logs a user in: checks the password file, sets the user,                 the environment and the motd; -l keeps SYS/wtmp  mmon           a timesharing monitor for a terminal or Hayes-modem line:                 answers, sets the speed and runs logon, as configured in                 SYS/mmon.config *watch          watches a modem line for RING and starts a command on an                 incoming call.  Super-user only *timeout        logon's idle timer: it ends a user's session when the                 time SYS/password allows runs out; logon runs it  errlog         a daemon that collects error messages into one file,                 SYS/errorlog on /h0  erradm         starts, stops, flushes and prints errlog's log *vcron          the cron daemon: runs each user's crontab.  Only members                 of group Cron may start it; start it from startup with & *crontab        lists, installs, edits and removes a user's crontab *mesg           sets your flags in SYS/utmp: a capital letter turns one                 on, a small one off.  M takes messages, V lets other                 users see you *mmenu          a menu to give users instead of a shell, as on a BBS:                 menus, help and jumps read from files in its menu                 directory, with a log of what each user ran *msg            sends a line to a user who is logged in; mmenu runs it                 for you.  It prints no usage *mwb            writes and edits manual entries in the proff format                 TOP's manuals use, kept under USR/DOC/.MAN on /h0 *newgrp         changes your group, as SYS/group allows, asking for the                 group's password if it has one *speak          a chat between two logged-in users on a split screen;                 Ctrl-C or Ctrl-E leaves *uid            prints your user and group ids, or another user's from                 SYS/password: `uid tester'.  Its refusal is in German------------------------------------------------------/dd/CMDS/REBUILT -- alternates kept beside the originals------------------------------------------------------  Alternates for names this disk already uses, other editions of the  same program, and builds for other processors.  The curated binary  is still the one in /dd/CMDS; these are here so you can compare or  swap them yourself.  The gzip set is for other CPUs.  CMDS/gzip is 1.2.2 for the 68000.  These are 1.2.4 built six ways -- 68000, 68020 and CPU32, each with  and without csl.  If your machine is a 68020 or a CPU32, one of  these is built for it and CMDS/gzip is not.  compress_4.0   compress 4.0, another edition of CMDS/compress  zoo_2.1        zoo 2.1 (1991), newer than the 2.01 in CMDS; it reads what                 2.01 writes and has an extended help under `zoo_2.1 H' *gzip68k_csl    gzip 1.2.4, 68000, needs csl *gzip020_csl    gzip 1.2.4, 68020, needs csl *gz